          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The tests use `Luhn::from(...)` with `&str`, `String`, `u8`, `u16`, `u32`, `u64`, and `usize`. The hint says \"your solution will fail if a new type is presented. Perhaps there exists a better solution?\" — the idiomatic solution is to implement `From<T> for Luhn` for all types that implement `ToString` (or `Display`), via a blanket impl:\n\n```rust\nimpl<T: ToString> From<T> for Luhn {\n    fn from(input: T) -> Self {\n        Self { digits: input.to_string() }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut wait — the tests call `Luhn::from(\"046 454 286\")` with `&str`. `&str: ToString` holds. `String: ToString` holds. `u8`, `u16`, etc. all implement `ToString`. `usize` too. So a blanket impl over `T: ToString` works and satisfies the hint.\n\nNote: the tests are marked `#[ignore]` except the first one. But the task says \"make the tests in tests/luhn-from.rs pass\" — probably run with `--include-ignored`. The blanket impl covers everything.\n\nLuhn algorithm:\n- Strip spaces (and other whitespace? The original Luhn exercise strips spaces. Here, non-digits are invalid, but spaces are allowed).\n- If there are fewer than 2 digits, invalid.\n- If any character is a non-digit (other than spaces), invalid.\n- Double every second digit from the right (i.e., starting from the second-to-last digit and moving left), subtract 9 if the result is greater than 9.\n- Sum all digits, and if the total is divisible by 10, valid.\n\nLet's write the implementation:\n\n```rust\npub struct Luhn {\n    digits: String,\n}\n\nimpl<T: ToString> From<T> for Luhn {\n    fn from(input: T) -> Self {\n        Luhn { digits: input.to_string() }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Luhn {\n    pub fn is_valid(&self) -> bool {\n        let digits: Vec<u32> = self\n            .digits\n            .chars()\n            .filter(|c| !c.is_whitespace())\n            .map(|c| c.to_digit(10))\n            .collect::<Option<Vec<_>>>()?\n            .into_iter()\n            .collect();\n        ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, `c.to_digit(10)` returns an Option. If it's None, invalid. Let's write it cleanly:\n\n```rust\nimpl Luhn {\n    pub fn is_valid(&self) -> bool {\n        let mut sum = 0u32;\n        let mut count = 0u32;\n        for c in self.digits.chars().rev() {\n            if c.is_whitespace() {\n                continue;\n            }\n            match c.to_digit(10) {\n                Some(d) => {\n                    count += 1;\n                    if count % 2 == 0 {\n                        let doubled = d * 2;\n                        sum += if doubled > 9 { doubled - 9 } else { doubled };\n                    } else {\n                        sum += d;\n                    }\n                }\n                None => return false,\n            }\n        }\n        count > 1 && sum % 10 == 0\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis handles everything. Note: the original Luhn exercise also treats \"00\" as invalid (single_zero_string_is_invalid is for \"0\", but in the original exercise \"00\" is also invalid). Here the tests only have \"0\". But count > 1 handles both.\n\nLet's check OFFLINE_CRATES.md to see if anything is needed — no, no dependencies are needed.\n\nNow let's write the file and run the tests with --include-ignored.\n",
